Explanation:
Tristram experiences a series of comic mishaps symbolizing life’s absurdity:  
His conception is interrupted by a clock-winding remark;  
his nose is crushed at birth by Dr. Slop’s forceps;  
his naming is botched due to miscommunication;  
and he later circumcises himself accidentally with a window sash.  
Each episode parodies human folly and philosophical pretension in Sterne’s digressive narrative.

The correct characters in Sterne’s "Tristram Shandy" include:  
Walter Shandy, the pedantic philosopher-father;  
Uncle Toby, the tender-hearted war veteran;  
Yorick, the benevolent clergyman later featured in "A Sentimental Journey";  
and Dr. Slop, the clumsy obstetrician whose forceps injury deforms Tristram’s nose.  
Tom Jones belongs to Henry Fielding’s novel and is not a Sterne character.

